{"kind":"report","text":"Integrated-product advice (intermediate, not the formal final review). Inspected exact commit 8a151a64b824983917785f03664ceb004a7d2f4f and the complete branch delta from 5d18fa8; go test ./..., go vet ./..., and scripts/demo.sh all pass. I also reviewed the successor commit 044d4dc5921a62cb43ec8db5057732a5ef0de17e already on the branch: it correctly closes the acceptance/activation race by capturing consumers and enqueuing under the engine lock and draining under that lock before the revision switch — fold it into the final head.\n\nPRIOR FINDINGS — all closed. Milestone A: comma-join refusal and wildcard rejection hold. Milestone B: sum/histogram aggregation temporality and is_monotonic are now preserved in the canonical metric identity (B1), and TestCanonicalBytesAndDigestAreStable pins byte stability (B2). All milestone B advisories landed: per-connection foreign_keys/trusted_schema, settle() idempotent when the record is already deleted, running capacity counters in inbox_state. The agent-guard insufficient-telemetry finding is now deduplicated per harness/session rather than per event.\n\nCONDITIONS CONFIRMED. CAS lifecycle: draft mutations, validation, and activation all compare-and-swap on the draft revision (registry UPDATE ... WHERE draft_revision=expected). Continue/reset boundaries: continue requires a live projection and storage compatibility; reset builds a candidate database, swaps atomically with backup-restore on rename failure, and starts at the drained boundary. Projection-before-obligation recovery: Process treats a replayed position with matching event ID as already applied, and the recovery test proves no duplicate evaluation. Runtime upgrade: a profile-mismatched active revision holds OTLP acceptance closed (consumer snapshot fails) while control and query stay up, and activation under the new profile detaches stale obligations. Gap isolation: a failing tailapp gaps and detaches alone, tested. Query surface: default-deny authorizer over a read-only attached sandbox with defensive config, statement/limit/interrupt bounds, single-statement enforcement, export-view-only mount access via innermost-view attribution, frontier alignment enforcement, and frontier_changed refusals. CLI and MCP both adapt the one control service over the private 0600 socket; the eleven MCP tools match the spec, and the query tool's description says detective observation, not inline prevention. Immediate value: the demo proves cross-harness violations, explicit unknowns, loop findings, the caller-cutoff stalled query, and joined exports through both CLI and MCP.\n\nBLOCKING CORRECTIONS before the final artifact:\n\nB1 — every Process error opens a permanent gap, including transient failures. projection.Process records a gap in its deferred handler for any error, and engine.Drain then detaches all obligations. Context cancellation is one such error: Activate drains with the caller's request context, so a client disconnect mid-drain can permanently gap a healthy tailapp and discard its future records. Classify failures: deterministic evaluation, validation, and constraint errors gap; context cancellation and transient SQLite errors must roll back and leave the obligation pending for retry.\n\nB2 — recovery is engine-fatal instead of app-unavailable. Engine.Open fails entirely if any single app's projection is missing, corrupt, or identity-mismatched, but the approved design makes that condition per-app unavailable while the engine serves everyone else. This composes badly with activation ordering: reset swaps the database files and only then records registry activation, so a crash in that window leaves projection identity ahead of the registry — and recover() then refuses to start the engine at all, with manual file surgery the only exit. Make recovery mark the app unavailable (excluded from consumers, queries refused with a stable code) and reconcile or journal the reset swap so restart always chooses one complete state.\n\nB3 — the design's gap-repair path cannot work. The architecture promises repair by schema-compatible reactivation resuming with future events, but Continue() never clears gap_position or advances the frontier, and consumers() excludes gapped apps — so a gapped tailapp can only be repaired by reset, destroying all its materialized state. Continue-activation of a gapped app must clear the gap and set the frontier to the new drained boundary. There is no test for gap repair; add one alongside TestGapDetachesOnlyFailingTailapp.\n\nADVISORIES, non-blocking but worth doing before publication:\n\nA1 — reset renames the old database to state.retired.\u003cns\u003e.sqlite and keeps it forever. The user acknowledged discard; retaining telemetry-derived tables indefinitely is a privacy surprise. Delete after a successful switch, or document the retention and clean it on init.\n\nA2 — a failed reset can leave state.candidate.sqlite behind, and every later reset then fails with \"activation candidate already exists\". Remove stale candidates on failure and on startup.\n\nA3 — Drain holds the engine mutex for the entire backlog, so queries, status, and lifecycle calls block until the whole queue drains, and Close waits unboundedly. Bound each drain pass (some number of waves per lock acquisition) so the query barrier sits between waves as designed.\n\nA4 — the query result reports delivery_head as the primary's interpreted position, hiding ingestion lag; wire the inbox's true delivery head through so agents can see how far behind they are reading.\n\nA5 — an export whose defining query reads an application view compiles, but at query time the authorizer sees the application view as the innermost view and denies the read. Either refuse view-based exports at compile time or authorize through the view chain; add a test either way.\n\nA6 — mounted exports are only rewritten in FROM/JOIN position and comma-listed relations are denied; document that callers must alias mounts in FROM/JOIN and reference columns through the alias.\n\nA7 — a JSON-typed column whose value is a bare number is stored with numeric affinity by sqliteValue, and fromSQLite then rejects it as invalid JSON storage on the next read. Encode every JSON-typed value as JSON text regardless of its top-level type.\n\nA8 — guard.jsonata now counts any non-success event as no-progress, including success=null from gated telemetry, so a harness that never exports success generates bounded-no-progress findings on healthy sessions. Gate the no-progress counter on observed success (or record unknown), consistent with the coverage-honesty contract.\n\nA9 — milestone D's expected-revision race test and an acceptance test for the runtime-upgrade hold (spec acceptance 17) are not yet present; both are cheap while the code is fresh.\n\nThe product is close: the immediate-value story is real and demonstrated, prior corrections were all applied with tests, and the remaining blockers are localized to failure classification, recovery, and gap repair — exactly the paths a demo never exercises but the first real crash will. Fix B1-B3, fold in 044d4dc5, and I expect the formal exact-head review to approve."}